<style>
    .faq-section-container {
        padding: 60px 20px;
        max-width: 1000px;
        margin: 0 auto;
        background: #fff;
    }

    .faq-wrapper {
        text-align: center;
    }

    .section-title {
        font-size: 36px;
        font-weight: 600;
        color: #D8A26C;
        margin-bottom: 50px;
        letter-spacing: 1px;
    }

    .faq-list {
        display: flex;
        flex-direction: column;
        gap: 40px;
    }

    .faq-group {
        text-align: left;
    }

    .group-title {
        font-size: 24px;
        font-weight: 600;
        color: #D8A26C;
        margin-bottom: 20px;
        padding-bottom: 10px;
        border-bottom: 2px solid #D8A26C;
    }

    .faq-items {
        display: flex;
        flex-direction: column;
        gap: 15px;
    }

    .faq-item {
        border-bottom: 1px solid #f0f0f0;
    }

    .faq-details {
        width: 100%;
    }

    .faq-question {
        width: 100%;
        padding: 20px 0;
        background: none;
        border: none;
        text-align: left;
        font-size: 16px;
        font-weight: 500;
        color: #333;
        cursor: pointer;
        display: flex;
        justify-content: space-between;
        align-items: center;
        transition: color 0.3s ease;
        list-style: none;
    }

    .faq-question:hover {
        color: #D8A26C;
    }

    .faq-question::-webkit-details-marker {
        display: none;
    }

    .faq-details[open] .faq-question {
        color: #D8A26C;
    }

    .faq-details[open] .chevron-icon {
        transform: rotate(180deg);
    }

    .chevron-icon {
        transition: transform 0.3s ease;
        color: #D8A26C;
    }

    .faq-answer {
        padding: 0 0 20px 0;
        color: #666;
        line-height: 1.8;
        font-size: 15px;
    }

    .faq-answer p {
        margin: 0 0 10px 0;
    }

    .faq-answer ul {
        margin: 10px 0;
        padding-left: 25px;
    }

    .faq-answer li {
        margin-bottom: 5px;
        color: #555;
    }

    @media (max-width: 768px) {
        .faq-section-container {
            padding: 40px 15px;
        }

        .section-title {
            font-size: 28px;
            margin-bottom: 30px;
        }

        .group-title {
            font-size: 20px;
        }

        .faq-question {
            font-size: 15px;
            padding: 15px 0;
        }

        .faq-answer {
            font-size: 14px;
            padding: 0 0 15px 0;
        }
    }
</style>

{% schema %}
{
  "name": "Baking Knowledge Q&A",
  "settings": [
    {
      "type": "text",
      "id": "title",
      "label": "Section Title",
      "default": "Baking Knowledge Q&A"
    },
    {
      "type": "header",
      "content": "Flour Knowledge"
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "id": "flour_q1",
      "label": "Flour Mixing Question",
      "default": "Question 001: If I don't have flour with the required gluten level at home, how can I mix it myself?"
    },
    {
      "type": "textarea",
      "id": "flour_a1",
      "label": "Flour Mixing Answer",
      "default": "High-gluten flour + low-gluten flour 1:1 mix = medium-gluten flour;\nHigh-gluten flour + cornstarch 4:1 mix = medium-gluten flour;\nMedium-gluten flour + cornstarch 4:1 mix = low-gluten flour;\nHigh-gluten flour + cornstarch 1:1 mix = low-gluten flour\nMedium-gluten flour can also increase gluten by adding vital wheat gluten, and the specific ratio may vary by brand."
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "id": "flour_q2",
      "label": "Flour Classification Question",
      "default": "Question 034: What are the main classifications of flour?"
    },
    {
      "type": "textarea",
      "id": "flour_a2",
      "label": "Flour Classification Answer",
      "default": "Currently, the most commonly used classification is based on protein content: high-gluten flour, medium-gluten flour, and low-gluten flour."
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "id": "flour_q3",
      "label": "Low-Gluten Flour Question",
      "default": "Question 035: What is low-gluten flour?"
    },
    {
      "type": "textarea",
      "id": "flour_a3",
      "label": "Low-Gluten Flour Answer",
      "default": "Low-gluten flour is also called cake flour. It is mainly used for various cakes and small pastries. Its protein content is 6.5%~8.5%. It is pure white in color, easy to knead into a dough, and easy to shape."
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "id": "flour_q4",
      "label": "Medium-Gluten Flour Question",
      "default": "Question 036: What is medium-gluten flour?"
    },
    {
      "type": "textarea",
      "id": "flour_a4",
      "label": "Medium-Gluten Flour Answer",
      "default": "Medium-gluten flour is what we call all-purpose flour. Protein content is 8.0%~10.5%. Suitable for steamed buns, dumplings, pancakes, and other Chinese pastries. It has a milky white color, semi-loose texture, balanced gluten and viscosity."
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "id": "flour_q5",
      "label": "High-Gluten Flour Question",
      "default": "Question 037: What is high-gluten flour?"
    },
    {
      "type": "textarea",
      "id": "flour_a5",
      "label": "High-Gluten Flour Answer",
      "default": "Protein content is 11.5%~14.5%. Strong gluten, high viscosity, suitable for bread, noodles, etc. It has a darker color and smooth texture. Grab a handful of high-gluten flour, squeeze it with your fist and release, it will quickly disperse and not easily form a dough."
    },
    {
      "type": "header",
      "content": "Eggs and Dairy Products"
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "id": "egg_q1",
      "label": "Egg Size Question",
      "default": "Question 002: What size are the eggs mentioned in baking recipes?"
    },
    {
      "type": "textarea",
      "id": "egg_a1",
      "label": "Egg Size Answer",
      "default": "In baking, choose eggs with a shell weight of about 60 grams.\nThe optimal storage temperature for eggs is: refrigerated at 2-6°C. For making butter cookies, it is recommended to take the eggs out in advance to return to room temperature."
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "id": "milk_q1",
      "label": "Milk Type Question",
      "default": "Question 005: What kind of milk is used in baking?"
    },
    {
      "type": "textarea",
      "id": "milk_a1",
      "label": "Milk Type Answer",
      "default": "The milk in baking recipes refers to \"whole milk\", which can enhance the flavor of baked goods, making them moist, smooth, and rich in milky aroma."
    },
    {
      "type": "header",
      "content": "Butter and Oils"
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "id": "butter_q1",
      "label": "Butter Classification Question",
      "default": "Question 003: What are the classifications of butter in baking?"
    },
    {
      "type": "textarea",
      "id": "butter_a1",
      "label": "Butter Classification Answer",
      "default": "Butter comes in salted and unsalted varieties. Generally, unsalted butter is used in baking. If using salted butter, the amount of salt in the recipe needs to be reduced accordingly. However, since the salt content in different salted butters varies, it is not recommended to use salted butter in baking.\nAdditionally, butter is classified as animal butter and plant butter. From a health and taste perspective, animal butter is more recommended."
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "id": "oil_q1",
      "label": "Cake Oil Question",
      "default": "Question 004: What oils are referred to in cake recipes?"
    },
    {
      "type": "textarea",
      "id": "oil_a1",
